<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape Colony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1652">1652</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cape Colony was the settlement at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> established in 1652 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> as a resupply station for ships sailing to Asia. It grew into a settler colony whose descendants formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> community. The colony outlasted the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> there and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the early nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-colony.jpg" alt="Cape Colony" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Cape_Colony_(1876%E2%80%931910).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>An expedition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC) led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-riebeeck">Jan van Riebeeck</a> established a trading post and naval victualling station at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> in 1652. Its aim was a harbour of refuge for company ships on the long voyages between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Within about three decades the Cape held a large community of vrijburgers, or free citizens, former company employees who undertook to farm the land for at least twenty years in exchange for tax exemption and loans of tools and seeds. In 1688 the company also sponsored the immigration of nearly two hundred French Huguenot refugees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Colonists on the frontier, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a>, moved far beyond the colony's first borders, and some, the trekboers, became permanently nomadic. The period of company rule saw bitter, genocidal conflicts between the colonists and the Khoe-speaking peoples, and later the Xhosa, who were seen as competitors for farmland. The company also imported thousands of slaves from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> and other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. By the end of the eighteenth century the population included about 26,000 people of European descent and 30,000 slaves.</p><p>In 1795 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> occupied the Cape after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, defeating the company's militia at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muizenberg">Battle of Muizenberg</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Treaty of Amiens</a> returned it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> in 1803, but Britain took it again after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blaauwberg">Battle of Blaauwberg</a> and set up a colony on 8 January 1806. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> formally ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in 1814. British settlers arrived at Port Elizabeth in 1820, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished in 1834. Resentment among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> of this change and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a> and culture led many to trek inland in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-trek">Great Trek</a> and form the Boer Republics.</p><p>The colony became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a> with representative government in 1853 and elected its first parliament in 1854, with a franchise based on a property qualification regardless of race. It achieved responsible government in 1872 under its first Prime Minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-molteno">John Molteno</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The colony stretched from the Atlantic coast inland and eastward along the southern coast, about half of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, with its eastern boundary at the Fish River after several wars against the Xhosa. In 1910 it joined three other colonies to form the Union of South Africa and became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-province">Province of the Cape of Good Hope</a>. In 1994 the Cape Province was divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-cape">Northern Cape</a> and Western Cape, with smaller parts in North West province.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cape Colony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British <b>Cape Colony</b> was built on the settlement founded at Table Bay by the Dutch East India Company (VOC) in 1652.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Cape Colony</b>, established in 1652 by Jan van Riebeeck as a VOC resupply station, produced the Afrikaner settler community whose descendants shaped South African history into the twentieth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orange Free State took shape on the high grasslands between the Orange and Vaal rivers, where Voortrekkers and other Dutch-speaking farmers from the <b>Cape Colony</b> settled from the 1830s among Griqua, Barolong, Sotho and other communities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The South African Republic grew out of the Great Trek of the 1830s, when several thousand Dutch-speaking farmers, the Voortrekkers, left the <b>Cape Colony</b> to escape British rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Cape Colony</b>, the Natal Colony, the Transvaal Colony and the Orange River Colony became its four provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
